clone
Usage: clone

Copy this whole stick onto a spare you plug in.

	clone

xos lives on a usb stick, and a stick can be lost or wear out. clone
makes a spare: plug an empty usb stick into the same machine and run
clone with no arguments. It finds the one spare you plugged in, shows
you both sticks and their sizes, and makes you type the spare's model
back before it writes anything -- so it can never overwrite the wrong
disk.

It copies every byte: the boot code, the read-only system, and your
encrypted p3. Then it reads the copy back and checks it matches, byte
for byte, before it calls the copy done. The spare boots on the same
enrolled keys and unlocks p3 with the same passphrase, so guard it like
the original and store it somewhere apart.

It refuses if no spare is plugged in, if more than one is (leave only
the target), if the spare is smaller than this stick, or if the spare
is in use. For the cleanest copy, turn the write-protect switch ON
first (vault mode) so nothing can change while it copies.

See also: dd, sha256sum, scrub
